[THOUGHT] The EGD result is definitive: a gastric ulcer on the lesser curvature — a classic location for peptic ulcers, often associated with H. pylori infection or NSAID use. This finding anchors the diagnosis and explains the postprandial epigastric pain and unintentional weight loss. Biopsy was implied in the EGD request, but the Analyst only reported the anatomic finding, not histopathology or H. pylori status on biopsy. However, the presence of a gastric ulcer is itself a discriminating objective finding that establishes the diagnosis of peptic ulcer disease. All eight domains have now been addressed:  
1) Chief complaint & onset — epigastric discomfort/cramping, 2 hours prior (though likely chronic given weight loss);  
2) Course — postprandial worsening, stable over 2 hours;  
3) Associated symptoms — denied red flags except weight loss;  
4) PMH — unremarkable;  
5) Medications — probable NSAIDs;  
6) Social — smoking, occasional alcohol;  
7) Physical exam — no tenderness/masses;  
8) Objective test — EGD confirming gastric ulcer.  

No further information is needed to establish the most likely diagnosis: **peptic ulcer disease**, likely secondary to NSAID use and/or smoking, with possible H. pylori co-factor. The evidence is complete.

[FINISH]

